{"answer":"Medical knowledge graphs can support clinical LLMs by supplying structured knowledge, linking patient evidence to biomedical concepts, and making retrieved evidence traceable. One study finds that KG-judgment training with KL regularization makes sparse model updates and can improve reasoning, while task-specific fine-tuning produces denser updates [1]. MM-KG explicitly connects multimodal patient observations with biomedical relations, enabling query-conditioned retrieval for an LLM and traceable evidence use [2]. Other approaches emphasize evidence-linked citations and temporal disease information: HEG-TKG targets verifiable rare-disease claims [5], while ChronoMedKG retrieval improves performance on temporal clinical questions [6]. Results are not uniformly positive: KG grounding helps clinical QA chiefly when the decisive fact is outside the model’s training, rather than for already-known facts [3].","grounded":true}"}]} 
